\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The index formula can fail without graded hermiticity}
\date{2026-11-23}

\p{On #{\mathbb C^2} take an orthonormal basis #{u,w} and
#{a>0}. Set #{\Gamma u=u}, #{\Gamma w=-w}, #{\Gamma'u=u} and
#{\Gamma'w=-w+bu} for #{b\ne0}. The second involution has
square #{\bbI}, but #{\langle u,\Gamma'w\rangle} is nonzero
whereas #{\langle\Gamma'u,w\rangle=0}. The expansion in
\ref{fdip-014A} shows that #{D=a^{-1}(\bbI -\Gamma\Gamma')}
obeys the quadratic relation. Directly #{Du=0} and
#{Dw=-a^{-1}bu}, so its kernel is the positive line and its
graded index is #{1}. Both gradings have trace zero. Here the
second involution lacks self-adjointness, #{D} lacks graded
hermiticity, and the trace formula of \ref{fdip-014B} fails.}
